A Redis sorted set (`ZSET`) stores members with associated scores, automatically maintaining sort order. The **key operations** that make leaderboards efficient are built into the data structure itself.

`ZADD` adds or updates a score — **O(log N)** time complexity. `ZREVRANK` returns a member's rank (0-indexed, highest score first) — also O(log N). `ZREVRANGE` returns the top K members with scores — O(log N + K). `ZSCORE` returns a member's score — O(1). All operations that a leaderboard needs, all sub-millisecond for millions of members.

The implementation for a Spring Boot gaming platform uses these operations directly through the **Redis template**. The service wraps the sorted set commands into domain methods that map naturally to leaderboard requirements — updating scores, querying ranks, fetching top-N, and the critical "around me" view.

```java
@Service
public class LeaderboardService {
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private static final String KEY = "leaderboard:global";

    public void updateScore(String userId, double score) {
        redis.opsForZSet().add(KEY, userId, score);
    }

    public Long getRank(String userId) {
        return redis.opsForZSet().reverseRank(KEY, userId);
    }

    public Set<ZSetOperations.TypedTuple<String>> getTopN(int n) {
        return redis.opsForZSet()
            .reverseRangeWithScores(KEY, 0, n - 1);
    }
}
```

The `getAroundMe` feature is the non-obvious capability that interviewers look for — showing a player their rank with the 5 players above and below them. This is implemented as a single `ZREVRANGE` call with **computed offsets** from the user's current rank, making it O(log N) rather than requiring a separate query. The sorted set maintains order on every insert, so rank computation never requires a full re-sort.

The upload flow starts on the client. The client splits the file into **4MB chunks**, computes a **SHA-256 hash** of each chunk, and sends the hash list to the server. The server checks which hashes already exist in storage and responds with the subset the client needs to upload. Only **novel chunks** travel over the network.

This hash-first protocol achieves two types of deduplication simultaneously. **Same-user dedup**: when a user saves a new version of a document, most chunks are identical to the previous version — only changed chunks upload. **Cross-user dedup**: when two users upload the same file (common in corporate environments), the second upload discovers all chunks already exist and completes instantly.

**Fixed-size chunking** (every 4MB boundary) is the simple starting point. Its weakness: inserting a byte at the beginning shifts every subsequent boundary, making all chunks appear new. **Content-defined chunking** (Rabin fingerprinting) picks boundaries based on content patterns, so insertions only affect chunks immediately around the edit. Dropbox uses content-defined chunking in production.

The upload service implements the dedup check and storage.

```java
@Service
public class ChunkUploadService {
    private final S3Client s3;
    private final ChunkRepository chunkRepo;

    public List<String> findMissingChunks(List<String> clientHashes) {
        Set<String> existing = chunkRepo.findExistingHashes(clientHashes);
        return clientHashes.stream()
            .filter(h -> !existing.contains(h))
            .toList();
    }

    public void storeChunk(String sha256, byte[] data) {
        if (chunkRepo.existsByHash(sha256)) return;  // dedup
        s3.putObject("chunks-bucket", sha256, data);
        chunkRepo.save(new ChunkRecord(sha256, data.length));
    }
}
```

Dropbox reported approximately **25% storage savings** from cross-user deduplication alone, with significant additional savings from version-level dedup within individual users' file histories.

The metadata service manages titles, descriptions, channel information, and engagement counts. This layer is IOPS-bound rather than bandwidth-bound.

**View counting** at YouTube scale reveals a classic distributed systems problem. A viral video receiving millions of views per minute cannot use `UPDATE videos SET view_count = view_count + 1` — that single row becomes permanently lock-contended. The solution is **event buffering**: publish view events to Kafka, aggregate counts with a stream processor (Flink) in 30-second tumbling windows, and batch-update the database.

```java
@Service
public class ViewCountService {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, ViewEvent> kafka;

    public void recordView(String videoId, String userId) {
        kafka.send("video-views",
            new ViewEvent(videoId, userId, Instant.now()));
    }
}
```

The Flink consumer accumulates counts per video per 30-second window and writes a single batch update to PostgreSQL. This reduces write pressure from millions of individual increments to a few thousand batch operations per window. Display approximate counts ("1.2M views") refreshed periodically — users don't need per-second precision.

**Search** indexes video metadata in Elasticsearch — title, description, tags, and auto-generated captions from speech-to-text transcription. Search relevance combines text matching with engagement signals like view count, like ratio, and recency.

**Content moderation** runs as part of the transcoding DAG. ML models scan sampled frames for policy violations — nudity, violence, copyright-protected material. Flagged videos enter a human review queue and remain private until cleared. Audio/video fingerprinting (similar to YouTube's Content ID) matches uploads against a database of protected works to detect unauthorized use before publication.

Uber's **H3 library** divides the Earth's surface into hexagonal cells at multiple resolutions. Resolution 7 produces cells of approximately **5 km²** — a typical city contains a few hundred cells. Every driver's GPS coordinates map to a single cell ID.

When a driver sends a location update, the system computes the H3 cell, removes the driver from their previous cell's set, and adds them to the new cell's set — both **O(1) Redis commands**. When a rider requests a ride, the dispatch service computes the rider's cell and retrieves all drivers from that cell plus the **6 neighboring hexagons** (H3's `gridDisk(1)` function).

This reduces nearest-driver search from scanning 5 million global drivers to examining roughly **70–350 candidates** in 7 cells — three orders of magnitude fewer distance calculations.

```java
@Service
public class LocationService {
    private final H3Core h3;
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public void updateLocation(String driverId, double lat, double lng) {
        String newCell = h3.latLngToCellAddress(lat, lng, 7);
        String prevCell = redis.opsForValue()
            .getAndSet("driver:cell:" + driverId, newCell);
        if (prevCell != null && !prevCell.equals(newCell)) {
            redis.opsForSet().remove("cell:" + prevCell, driverId);
        }
        redis.opsForSet().add("cell:" + newCell, driverId);
    }

    public Set<String> findNearbyDrivers(double lat, double lng) {
        String riderCell = h3.latLngToCellAddress(lat, lng, 7);
        List<String> cells = h3.gridDisk(riderCell, 1);
        return cells.stream()
            .flatMap(c -> redis.opsForSet().members("cell:" + c).stream())
            .collect(Collectors.toSet());
    }
}
```

If the initial 7-cell search returns no available drivers, the system expands to `gridDisk(2)` — 19 cells covering a wider radius. Progressive expansion avoids unnecessary wide searches in dense areas while still finding drivers in sparse suburbs.

Finding the nearest driver by straight-line distance is misleading. A driver 2km away on a highway may arrive in 3 minutes, while a driver 500m away in gridlocked traffic takes 12 minutes. **ETA-based matching** produces dramatically better rider experience.

After the location service returns nearby candidates, the dispatch service filters to available drivers and calculates **estimated time of arrival** for each using a routing engine (OSRM or Google Maps Directions API). The best candidate has the lowest ETA, not the shortest distance.

```java
@Service
public class DispatchService {
    private final LocationService locationService;
    private final RoutingEngine routing;

    public Optional<DriverMatch> dispatch(RideRequest request) {
        Set<String> nearby = locationService
            .findNearbyDrivers(request.getPickupLat(), request.getPickupLng());

        return nearby.stream()
            .map(id -> driverService.getDriver(id))
            .filter(Driver::isAvailable)
            .map(d -> {
                Duration eta = routing.estimateETA(
                    d.getLat(), d.getLng(),
                    request.getPickupLat(), request.getPickupLng());
                return new DriverMatch(d, eta);
            })
            .min(Comparator.comparing(DriverMatch::getEta));
    }
}
```

The dispatch flow **offers** the ride to the best candidate with a **15-second acceptance timeout**. If the driver declines or doesn't respond, the next candidate is offered. After 3 failed offers, the matching re-runs from scratch because driver positions may have changed during the offer cycle.

**Double-dispatch prevention** is critical: two concurrent ride requests in the same area could both be offered to the same driver. The system places a **Redis lock** on the driver ID when an offer is extended — `SETNX driver:lock:{driverId}` with 15-second TTL. Other dispatch queries filter out locked drivers. If the offer expires without acceptance, the TTL auto-releases the lock and the driver becomes available again.
